---
const iconStyle =
  'width:2.6rem;height:2.6rem;border-radius:var(--mos-radius-sm);display:grid;place-items:center;background:var(--mos-color-accent-soft);color:var(--mos-color-accent)'
const linkStyle = 'font-size:0.9rem'
---

<section class="band" id="safe">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="section-head center">
      <span class="mos-eyebrow">Kept safe</span>
      <h2 class="mos-section-title">Looked after, so you don't have to think about it</h2>
      <p class="mos-body-lg mos-meta">Self-hosting only pays off if the thing hosting your life is careful. Here is the short version; the docs tell the whole story.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="paths">
      <div class="mos-panel path">
        <span class="ico" style={iconStyle}><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-layers"/></svg></span>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.15rem">The critical parts are tested by millions</h3>
        <p class="mos-meta" style="font-size:0.92rem">Ubuntu, Docker, Caddy, Let's Encrypt and restic do the heavy lifting; MOS adds the glue and the friendly screens. If those names mean nothing to you, that's fine. If they do, you know why they're here.</p>
        <a class="mos-link" href="/docs/reference/architecture/" style={linkStyle}>MOS architecture →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="mos-panel path">
        <span class="ico" style={iconStyle}><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-lock"/></svg></span>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.15rem">Your data is encrypted</h3>
        <p class="mos-meta" style="font-size:0.92rem">On your own hardware, everything sits in a standard LUKS vault that the machine's own security chip opens at boot. If the machine goes missing, its disk is unreadable anywhere else.</p>
        <a class="mos-link" href="/docs/install/own-hardware/#good-to-know" style={linkStyle}>How the vault works →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="mos-panel path">
        <span class="ico" style={iconStyle}><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-key"/></svg></span>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.15rem">Your whole suite in one backup</h3>
        <p class="mos-meta" style="font-size:0.92rem">Every app and everything in it, on a schedule, to a drive you plug in or to storage online, encrypted with your recovery key before it leaves the server. Plain <a class="mos-link" href="https://restic.net/" rel="noopener">restic</a> archives your key opens anywhere, and one button brings it all back.</p>
        <a class="mos-link" href="/docs/guides/backup-restore/" style={linkStyle}>Back up and restore →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="mos-panel path">
        <span class="ico" style={iconStyle}><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg></span>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.15rem">HTTPS, like magic</h3>
        <p class="mos-meta" style="font-size:0.92rem">A real, browser-trusted padlock on your home network, minutes after first start. Nothing to buy, no port to open, no terminal to touch. Don't believe us? The docs explain exactly how.</p>
        <a class="mos-link" href="/docs/install/easy-address/" style={linkStyle}>How the padlock works →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="mos-panel path">
        <span class="ico" style={iconStyle}><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-plug"/></svg></span>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.15rem">Updates you approve</h3>
        <p class="mos-meta" style="font-size:0.92rem">Apps and the platform each show what changes before you press the button, and a failed update puts things back. Ubuntu's own security patches install themselves.</p>
        <a class="mos-link" href="/docs/guides/updates/" style={linkStyle}>How updates work →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="mos-panel path">
        <span class="ico" style={iconStyle}><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-eye"/></svg></span>
        <h3 style="font-size:1.15rem">Every connection disclosed</h3>
        <p class="mos-meta" style="font-size:0.92rem">MOS has no telemetry and never reports back to us. If an app depends on an outside service, its page says exactly which one and why before you install, evidence included. Your server checks the catalog's signature before trusting a word of it.</p>
        <a class="mos-link" href="/docs/privacy/how-we-assess/" style={linkStyle}>How apps are assessed →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
